TI  - Coroners' recommendations following older adult homicide in Australia: a 
      cross-sectional study of frequency, nature and organisational responses.
LID - ip-2025-045870 [pii]
LID - 10.1136/ip-2025-045870 [doi]
AB  - BACKGROUND: Older adult homicide is a public health and social problem. 
      Recommendations made by coroners following investigation into these deaths 
      provide potentially critical insights into strategies for future prevention. 
      While previous studies have examined recommendations on domestic and family 
      homicides, there is a paucity of research specific to older adult homicide. 
      METHODS: This study sought to identify the frequency and examine the nature of 
      and responses to coroners' recommendations on adult victims of homicides aged 65 
      years and older during the period 2001-2018. Cases and coroners' recommendations 
      were identified using the National Coronial Information System and recommendation 
      responses were identified via publicly available sources. A pre-determined 
      framework was used to assess the presence and nature of seven public health 
      elements. RESULTS: There were 29 unique recommendations pertaining to nine older 
      adult homicide incidents. The public health elements 'countermeasure', 'level of 
      intervention' and 'implementation strategy' were present in all recommendations. 
      Identification of 'risk factors', the 'organisation directed the recommendation' 
      and the 'organisational response' was available for 27 (93%). The most frequently 
      stated 'level of intervention' was for policy or guidance material, and the 
      'implementation strategy' was commonly an environment, product or design change. 
      Recommendations were mostly directed to federal or state government departments, 
      such as the police/justice department. Most organisations supported the 
      recommendations (79%), with over half implementing or partially implementing, at 
      the time of the study. CONCLUSIONS: Research targeting older adult homicide 
      circumstances, as well as barriers to implementation, is crucial to extend the 
      preventative benefits of the coronial process.
