OAB - The opioid overdose epidemic is a national public health crisis. Naloxone is a 
      safe and effective tool that reverses overdose-related complications, and its 
      widespread distribution among first responders and laypeople is crucial for 
      preventing deaths from opioid overdoses nationally. Undergraduate and graduate 
      students who participate in naloxone training on campus can administer naloxone 
      on campus, in the city/region surrounding campus, in their home communities, and 
      in the cities/regions they might move to after graduation. While some college 
      campuses have implemented naloxone training, it is still not being done widely. 
      Our theory-informed research assesses student perceptions on reach, relevance, 
      and fit of naloxone training at Columbia University. Students (i) were generally 
      receptive to the training but perceived it as more relevant to the city 
      surrounding campus, potentially impacting engagement; (ii) were confused about 
      why the university was focusing on this training, given their perceived need for 
      improved access to campus substance use and mental health services more broadly; 
      and (iii) voiced the need for improved university-wide communications about the 
      importance of and its commitment to naloxone training. As more college campus 
      naloxone trainings emerge, combining such findings across campuses will be 
      critical for understanding how to improve adoption, implementation, and 
      sustainability of these trainings nationally.
