TI  - Post-traumatic Stress Disorder and Cardiovascular Disease.
PG  - 94
LID - 10.1007/s11886-016-0770-5 [doi]
AB  - Post-traumatic stress disorder (PTSD) is a disabling condition that develops 
      consequent to trauma exposure such as natural disasters, sexual assault, 
      automobile accidents, and combat that independently increases risk for early 
      incident cardiovascular disease (CVD) and cardiovascular (CV) mortality by over 
      50 % and incident hypertension risk by over 30 %. While the majority of research 
      on PTSD and CVD has concerned initially healthy civilian and military veteran 
      samples, emerging research is also demonstrating that PTSD consequent to the 
      trauma of an acute cardiac event significantly increases risk for early 
      recurrence and mortality and that patient experiences in the clinical pathway 
      that are related to the emergency department environment may provide an 
      opportunity to prevent PTSD onset and thus improve outcomes. Future directions 
      for clinical and implementation science concern broad PTSD and trauma screening 
      in the context of primary care medical environments and the testing of PTSD 
      treatments with CVD-related surrogates and endpoints.
